13. VALIDATION EVIDENCE
Every deterministic result should contain sufficient evidence to explain the evaluation.
Example:
{
  "observed_value": 850,
  "required_value": 900,
  "unit": "mm",
  "comparison": "GREATER_THAN_OR_EQUAL",
  "object_id": "uuid",
  "rule_code": "DOOR_CLEAR_WIDTH"
}
This permits the user and downstream systems to understand why the result occurred.

14. RULE SOURCE PROVENANCE
Where a validation rule originates from the Compliance & Regulatory Engine, the result shall preserve:
code_source_id
code_rule_id
code_manifest_id
citation
source_version
effective_date
The Validation Engine must never lose the regulatory provenance of a result.

15. CODE MANIFEST DEPENDENCY
Code-based validation shall execute against a specific:
code_manifest_id
The manifest identifies the applicable regulatory source set.
Therefore:
Project
 ↓
Jurisdiction
 ↓
Code Manifest
 ↓
Code Rules
 ↓
Validation Rules
 ↓
Validation Run
A validation result cannot be treated as jurisdictionally reproducible without the corresponding manifest.

16. PROJECT REQUIREMENT VALIDATION
Project requirements shall be validated independently from regulatory requirements.
Example:
Requirement:
Provide three bedrooms.

Model:
Two bedrooms.

Result:
FAIL
Another example:
Requirement:
Provide minimum 2,000 sq ft.

Model:
2,145 sq ft.

Result:
PASS
Project-specific requirements therefore remain independently traceable.

17. CONSTRAINT VALIDATION
Active project constraints shall be evaluated against the resulting model state.
Examples:
setback
maximum height
maximum area
lot coverage
floor-to-floor height
minimum room dimensions
required parking
required access
A model modification that violates a preserved constraint shall produce:
FAIL
and, where configured, prevent command finalization.

18. GEOMETRIC VALIDATION
The engine shall interface with the Geometry & Parametric Model Engine.
Geometric validation may include:
distance
clearance
containment
intersection
overlap
alignment
parallelism
perpendicularity
area
volume
height
offset
boundary relationship
The Validation Engine shall consume authoritative geometry outputs rather than independently creating competing project geometry.

19. GEOMETRY VERSION CONTROL
Every geometric validation shall identify:
project_id
model_version_id
object_id
geometry_version
A validation result generated from obsolete geometry shall not become the current validation state.

20. SPATIAL VALIDATION
Spatial validation evaluates relationships between spaces and architectural objects.
Examples:
room-to-room adjacency
required circulation
minimum room dimensions
required access path
space containment
space overlap
required clearances
The engine shall evaluate spatial relationships using normalized project-model data.

21. ACCESSIBILITY VALIDATION
Accessibility validation shall evaluate applicable requirements against:
doors
corridors
ramps
stairs
washrooms
turning spaces
clearances
accessible routes
parking
entrances
level changes
The applicable requirements originate from the project's jurisdictional code manifest and project requirements.
Where a condition requires professional interpretation, the result shall be:
REVIEW_REQUIRED
rather than automatically marked:
PASS

22. EGRESS VALIDATION
The engine shall support computational egress checks including:
exit count
exit distribution
travel distance
dead-end conditions
egress path continuity
door direction
egress width
occupant-load relationships
Where the governing rule cannot be safely reduced to deterministic computation, the result shall identify the condition for review.

23. FIRE AND LIFE-SAFETY VALIDATION
Initial categories include:
fire separations
rated assemblies
protected openings
exit systems
fire barriers
occupancy relationships
travel paths
required protection
The Validation Engine shall reference assembly properties from:
model.assemblies
and applicable regulatory rules from:
compliance.code_rules

24. ASSEMBLY VALIDATION
An assembly may be validated against:
fire rating
thermal performance
acoustic performance
thickness
layer configuration
manufacturer requirements
project specifications
Example:
Required fire rating:
2 HR

Assembly:
1 HR

Result:
FAIL

25. MODEL INTEGRITY VALIDATION
Before downstream drawing generation, the model must pass internal integrity checks.
Examples:
orphaned elements
missing levels
invalid parent references
duplicate identifiers
missing geometry
invalid geometry references
broken relationships
invalid assemblies
missing required parameters
Model integrity failures shall be treated as blocking conditions where downstream generation could produce unreliable documentation.

26. RELATIONSHIP VALIDATION
The engine shall validate the project dependency graph.
Examples:
door → host wall
window → host wall
space → level
level → building
element → project
drawing → view
view → model version
revision → model version
Broken dependencies shall produce:
FAIL
or:
BLOCKED
depending on whether the problem is a violated relationship or a missing prerequisite.

27. REQUIREMENT COVERAGE
The engine shall determine whether every mandatory project requirement has a validation state.
Each mandatory requirement should reach one of:
PASS
FAIL
REVIEW_REQUIRED
NOT_APPLICABLE
A mandatory requirement with no evaluation shall not silently disappear from the validation report.

28. VALIDATION COVERAGE
A validation run shall calculate:
total_rules
executed_rules
passed_rules
failed_rules
warning_rules
review_required_rules
blocked_rules
error_rules
The summary shall also calculate coverage:
validation_coverage =
executed_applicable_rules /
total_applicable_rules

29. VALIDATION GATES
The engine shall support validation gates.
Initial gates:
MODEL_GATE
GEOMETRY_GATE
COMPLIANCE_GATE
DOCUMENTATION_GATE
FINALIZATION_GATE
ACCEPTANCE_GATE
A gate defines the minimum validation conditions required to proceed to the next stage.

30. MODEL GATE
The Model Gate verifies:
model integrity
object relationships
required parameters
required geometry
project requirements
active constraints
The model must not proceed to authoritative documentation generation when critical model-integrity failures remain unresolved.

31. GEOMETRY GATE
The Geometry Gate verifies:
geometry completeness
geometry validity
geometry-version consistency
required spatial relationships
Geometry failures must be resolved before dependent drawings are generated.

32. COMPLIANCE GATE
The Compliance Gate evaluates the results generated from the applicable code manifest.
The gate identifies:
critical failures
high-severity failures
unresolved review items
blocked rules
missing regulatory inputs
The engine does not convert unresolved regulatory questions into automatic approval.

33. DOCUMENTATION GATE
The Documentation Gate verifies that the model contains sufficient information to generate the required drawing set.
Examples:
required views exist
required levels exist
drawing references resolve
sheet templates exist
annotations resolve
dimensions resolve
schedules resolve
title-block metadata exists

34. FINALIZATION GATE
The Finalization Gate verifies the complete provenance chain:
PROJECT
 ↓
MODEL VERSION
 ↓
REVISION
 ↓
CODE MANIFEST
 ↓
VALIDATION RUN
 ↓
DRAWING SET
 ↓
DOCUMENT PACKAGE
No finalized package may omit a required link in this chain.

35. ACCEPTANCE GATE
Customer acceptance occurs only after the required validation and documentation states have been recorded.
The acceptance process shall identify:
project_id
revision_id
model_version_id
validation_run_id
document_package_id
code_manifest_id
customer_id
This preserves the exact state accepted by the customer.

36. VALIDATION BLOCKING POLICY
The engine shall distinguish:
BLOCKING
NON_BLOCKING
REVIEW
INFORMATIONAL
A critical model integrity failure may be:
BLOCKING
A minor documentation warning may be:
NON_BLOCKING
A professional judgment condition may be:
REVIEW

