48. VALIDATION EXPLANATION
Every failure should be explainable through structured information.
Example:
Rule:
DOOR_CLEAR_WIDTH

Required:
900 mm

Observed:
850 mm

Difference:
-50 mm

Affected Object:
uuid

Status:
FAIL

Resolution:
Increase clear opening width to the applicable minimum.
The explanation layer may be rendered into natural language by Aria, but the underlying structured result remains authoritative.

49. VALIDATION RESOLUTION
A validation result may be associated with a corrective operation.
Conceptually:
validation result
 ↓
affected object
 ↓
recommended correction
 ↓
Aria command
 ↓
new model version
 ↓
new revision
 ↓
new validation run
The engine must not mutate historical results when a correction is made.

50. HISTORICAL VALIDATION
Every validation run remains associated with its original:
revision
model version
code manifest
rule versions
engine versions
A subsequent correction creates a new validation run.
Historical validation results remain intact.

51. VALIDATION COMPARISON
The engine shall support comparison between validation runs.
Example:
Revision 12
FAIL = 14

Revision 13
FAIL = 5
The comparison shall identify:
resolved issues
new issues
unchanged issues
regressed issues

52. REGRESSION DETECTION
A new revision shall be checked for regressions.
Example:
Revision 12:
Door D-101 = PASS

Revision 13:
Door D-101 = FAIL
The system identifies this as:
REGRESSION
Regression results shall be included in the revision validation summary.

53. AFFECTED-OBJECT ANALYSIS
When an object changes, the Validation Engine should determine which rules may be affected.
Example:
Wall W-201 changed
 ↓
Door D-201 hosted by W-201
 ↓
Room R-201 bounded by W-201
 ↓
Egress path affected
 ↓
Applicable validation rules queued
This allows incremental validation.

54. INCREMENTAL VALIDATION
The engine shall support:
FULL_VALIDATION
and:
INCREMENTAL_VALIDATION
Full validation evaluates the complete applicable rule set.
Incremental validation evaluates the affected dependency graph.
Finalization may require a full validation depending on project configuration.

55. VALIDATION DEPENDENCY GRAPH
Rules may declare dependencies:
rule A
 ↓
rule B
 ↓
rule C
Example:
occupant load
 ↓
required egress capacity
 ↓
required exit width
 ↓
door validation
A dependent rule may become:
BLOCKED
when a prerequisite rule fails or required data is unavailable.

